pages.text:
SHELF SAMPLES / 02

Return is not decoration

With an arrow function, an expression body returns its expression. A block body needs an explicit return statement.

const a = [1, 2].map(n => n * 2);
// [2, 4]

const b = [1, 2].map(n => { n * 2; });
// [undefined, undefined]

The multiplication in the second example happens, but its value is not returned by the callback. Map collects the callback results.

Close the document and explain this bug out loud before checking the code again.

SHELF  /  ORIGINAL SAMPLE NOTES 2

SHELF SAMPLES / 03

A closure keeps access

A closure is a function together with access to its surrounding lexical environment. The function can keep accessing that environment after the outer call has returned.

function counter() {
let value = 0;
return () => {
value += 1;
return value;
};
}
const next = counter();
next(); // 1
next(); // 2

Two separate calls to counter() create two independent environments.

SHELF  /  ORIGINAL SAMPLE NOTES 3

